# Captured request bodies: they contain an e-mail address, device_id,
# account_uuid and Anthropic's system prompt. They do not belong in the repo —
# tools/synthetic.py produces everything needed without a capture.
bodies/
*-raw.json
*-sent.json
*-profile.json
*-answer.txt
*-roh.json
*-gesendet.json
*-profil.json
*-antwort.txt

# Secrets
*.token
*.secret
llm-gateway.env
cc-gateway.env
.env.local

# This machine's own answers: where the models live, what the tunnel is called.
# Written by setup/install.sh from setup/local.env.template, which IS tracked.
# It normally lives in ~/.config and so never reaches the repo — this line is
# for the case where somebody points $LLM_STACK_ENV at a copy inside it.
llm-stack.env

# Runtime
*.log
bench/reports/*/server.log
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Slot states (546 MB each)
*.bin

# Cloudflare credentials — they belong in ~/.cloudflared, never in the repo
*.cloudflared.json
cert.pem
config.yml

# Named access — belongs in ~/.config, never in the repo. The cc-* twins are
# the pre-09/2026 names; kept so a copy from an old installation stays out too.
llm-gateway-tokens
cc-gateway-tokens

# Saved slot states (628 MB each)
llama-slots/

# --- internal: the maintainer's own working files -------------------------
#
# Kept on disk, deliberately NOT published. These are the tools and the log of
# DEVELOPING this stack, not of running it — a user who clones the repo to set
# up their own machine needs none of them, and a 2,000-line session log is
# noise in a repository whose point is that its documents are short and
# measured.
#
# Note what this does NOT do: a file already committed stays in the git
# HISTORY. This keeps it out of a fresh clone and out of the file browser,
# which is the point; it is not deletion. The history itself is squashed
# separately before publishing.
#
# tests/test_watchflashnext.py goes with the script it tests — a public test
# importing a module that is not in the clone is a red CI run on day one.
docs/HANDOVER.md
docs/HANDOVER-LOG.md
docs/FLASHNEXT-PLAN.md
setup/scripts/watchflashnext.py
setup/scripts/watch-flashnext.sh
tests/test_watchflashnext.py

# The German originals of the measurement records. The published versions are
# English; these are kept so that any figure in a translation can be checked
# against the text it came from. Translating does not change a measurement —
# but a translator can mistype one, and on 27.08. one did: three quoted Claude
# Code counter messages were retyped instead of copied, and the check that
# compares every number in both files is what caught it.
docs/measurements/*.de.md

# The machine documents: five German files describing this one machine — a
# dated state record, a runbook, the setup path, a glossary, a note on the
# shared NTFS partition. They are the SOURCE MATERIAL from which docs/setup/
# and the translated measurement records were made, and they are the
# maintainer's own working copies now.
#
# Making them internal was work rather than a deletion: everything published
# that cited them had to be repointed at what actually carries the evidence.
# tests/test_docs.py fails if a published link or command names one again.
docs/px13/

# docs/archive/ — withdrawn predecessors, surveys and .orig copies.
# Kept on disk, deliberately NOT published: nothing in it is needed to set up a
# Strix Halo or to get this stack running, and some of it is actively wrong —
# px13-einrichtung.html carries the amdgpu.gttsize recommendation that runbook
# rev. 7 revoked. Note what this does NOT do: files already committed stay in
# the git HISTORY. This keeps them out of a clone and out of the file browser,
# which is the point; it is not deletion.
docs/archive/

